wlanpi-mcp (0.6.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * capture: remove the hard 2000-record cap on capture_scan/capture_observe.
    max_frames is now caller-controlled with no ceiling: a positive value
    returns up to that many per-frame records, 0 returns counts only, and a
    negative value returns every frame (no cap). Per-kind counts remain exact
    regardless. For a genuinely complete capture prefer the file-capture tools
    over an unbounded capture_scan.

 -- Jake Snyder <jsnyder81@gmail.com>  Fri, 04 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

wlanpi-mcp (0.6.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * capture: fetch_pcap_file/stop_pcap_file now take 'capture_id' instead of
    'session_id'. The old name collided with the MCP SSE transport's reserved
    routing query param (POST /messages/?session_id=...), and some clients drop
    a tool argument sharing that name before it reaches the server - so a fetch
    by session_id failed with "pass session_id or path" while fetch by path
    worked. 'session_id' is kept as a back-compat alias; every result now
    carries 'capture_id'.
  * capture: bytes_written now advances while a file capture is running,
    instead of staying 0 until completion, so it is usable for progress.

 -- Jake Snyder <jsnyder81@gmail.com>  Fri, 04 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

wlanpi-mcp (0.6.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * capture: make file-backed captures resolvable by session_id from disk, not
    just from the in-memory registry. The pcapng filename now embeds the
    session_id (capture-<stamp>-<session_id>.pcapng), so fetch_pcap_file and
    stop_pcap_file still find a capture by session_id after the registry is
    gone (e.g. a server restart), and list_pcap_files also lists files left on
    disk from an earlier run (status 'on_disk'). Fixes fetch_pcap_file needing
    an explicit path once the server had restarted.

 -- Jake Snyder <jsnyder81@gmail.com>  Fri, 04 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

wlanpi-mcp (0.6.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * capture: add a file-backed capture family alongside the streaming tools.
    start_pcap_file starts a capture, hands the owning WebSocket to a
    background task that writes the raw pcapng byte stream to a managed
    directory on the device, and returns immediately with the session_id and
    path - so a capture can run well past the 60 s streaming window.
    stop_pcap_file ends one early, list_pcap_files shows what is running or
    done, and fetch_pcap_file returns the file to the client as an MCP blob
    (application/vnd.tcpdump.pcapng) for analysis in Wireshark/tshark.
  * capture: this deliberately relaxes two invariants (raw pcap, and a local
    file) scoped to Settings.PCAP_CAPTURE_DIR (default /tmp/wlanpi-mcp/
    captures); fetch_pcap_file refuses any path outside it. Every byte still
    comes from the core capture WebSocket - no subprocess, same JWT. Adds
    PCAP_CAPTURE_DIR and PCAP_MAX_DURATION_S (default 3600 s) settings.

 -- Jake Snyder <jsnyder81@gmail.com>  Fri, 04 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

wlanpi-mcp (0.5.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * capture: dissect the QBSS/BSS Load element (IE tag 11) from beacons and
    probe-responses. Each AP row now carries 'stations' (the associated
    client count) and 'channel_utilization' (percent), when the AP
    advertises the element. Merged across beacons so a later frame without
    the element does not clear it.
  * packaging: pin the dh_virtualenv interpreter to python3.13 (the
    requires-python floor) instead of the host default python3, which can
    drift ahead of the device (Ubuntu 26.04 defaults to 3.14) and yield a
    venv whose compiled wheels will not load on the 3.13 device.

